<!-- DEMO -->
<section id="demo">
  <div class="section-label">How It Works</div>
  <div class="section-title">Type the alarm.<br/>Get the fix.</div>
  <p class="section-sub">Describe your fault or paste an alarm code. FieldFix AI gives you root causes, test procedures, and step-by-step repair instructions instantly.</p>

  <div class="demo-grid">
    <div class="demo-left">
      <div class="demo-chat">
        <div class="demo-chat-header">
          <div class="demo-chat-icon">⚙️</div>
          <div class="demo-chat-title">FieldFix AI</div>
          <div class="demo-chat-status"><div class="demo-chat-status-dot"></div>Online</div>
        </div>
        <div class="demo-messages">
          <div class="dmsg user">
            <div class="dav user">👤</div>
            <div class="dbub user">Fanuc 0i-MD alarm SV0023 on X axis, machine won't move</div>
          </div>
          <div class="dmsg">
            <div class="dav ai">⚙️</div>
            <div class="dbub ai">
              <strong>SV0023 — Current Alarm (High)</strong><br/><br/>
              Root causes (high → low probability):<br/>
              1. Servo amplifier overcurrent — check amp LED status<br/>
              2. Motor winding short — measure phase resistance<br/>
              3. Encoder cable damaged — check for continuity<br/><br/>
              <strong>Step 1:</strong> Press SYSTEM → check SV diagnostic page for DC bus voltage. Should read 280-320VDC.<br/>
              <strong>Step 2:</strong> Power off. Measure motor phases U-V-W with ohmmeter — expect 2-8Ω, all equal.<br/>
              <strong>Step 3:</strong> Check encoder connector at motor for bent pins or moisture.<br/><br/>
              Tools: Multimeter (DC 600V range), ohmmeter<br/>
              Est. repair time: 30-90 min
